error: `slug` is the part of the type URI after `base`, so it is a non-empty run of the characters a URI path admits, written percent-encoded as it is sent
  --> tests/ui/macros/api_error_slug_refusals.rs:16:56
   |
16 | #[problem(base = "https://errors.example.com/", slug = "post removed", status = 410)]
   |                                                        ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^

error: `slug` is joined onto `base` verbatim, as the kebab-cased name is, so `base` carries the separator; a leading `/` under a `base` ending in one publishes an empty path segment, a type URI other than the one a sibling's `slug` without it names. Drop the leading `/`
  --> tests/ui/macros/api_error_slug_refusals.rs:21:56
   |
21 | #[problem(base = "https://errors.example.com/", slug = "/removed", status = 410)]
   |                                                        ^^^^^^^^^^

error: a `slug` ending in `/` publishes a type URI other than the same slug without it, as a kebab-cased name never ends in one. Drop the trailing `/`
  --> tests/ui/macros/api_error_slug_refusals.rs:26:56
   |
26 | #[problem(base = "https://errors.example.com/", slug = "removed/", status = 410)]
   |                                                        ^^^^^^^^^^

error: `//` in a `slug` is an empty path segment, publishing a type URI other than the one naming it with a single `/`. Write a single `/` between segments
  --> tests/ui/macros/api_error_slug_refusals.rs:31:56
   |
31 | #[problem(base = "https://errors.example.com/", slug = "moderation//removed", status = 410)]
   |                                                        ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^

error: `.` and `..` in a `slug`, percent-encoded as `%2E` or not, are dot segments, which RFC 3986 reference resolution removes, so a client resolving the type URI reads another one. Name the segment
  --> tests/ui/macros/api_error_slug_refusals.rs:36:56
